  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 214 AM EDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nSurface high pressure slowly slides eastward today from the southern \nGreat Lakes and then a couple more surface high pressure systems \npass by to the north of the Great Lakes Tuesday and Thursday. \nMeanwhile, mid level ridging continues to keep the area within a \nlarge scale area of subsidence quelling any large scale area of rain \nthrough this week and probably through next weekend. This is even as \na cold front stalls around the area Monday night/Tuesday and another \ncold front moves through later Wednesday/Thursday AM time frame. \nThese FROPAs will be dry FROPAs. Troughs attempt to make their way \neastward but continue to run into the stout mid level ridge east of \nthe Rockies through this work week. As such, various vort \nmaxes/surface lows take a northeast trek from the Plains into the \nwestern Great Lakes/southcentral Canada area. Attempts by WAA \nto affect the area cause a slow warming trend through the 60s \ntoday and Monday, reaching the 70s for highs on Tuesday \nsouthwest of US-30 through this week. Daily lows look to trend \nwarmer as well beyond this morning. There's a marginal chance \nfor frost Monday morning with about 3 hours of 38 degree temps \nor less, especially south of US-24. However, another \nfrost/freeze issuance will depend on if the growing season \ncontinues or if this morning's temps ended things. The area is \nfairly dry with MinRH values dipping into the 20s and 30s of \npercents today through Tuesday, but low level lapse rates aren't\nas strong as they could be, and even more of a factor, wind \ngusts are below considerable thresholds. Future dry days are \npossible beyond Tuesday, but wind gusts don't look respectable \nat this time. It is also possible our forecast dew points are \ntoo high as currently constituted beyond Tuesday.",
